> 1\. Around 19.5 million people (41 percent of the population) are facing high levels of acute food insecurity 

> 2\. Severe acute malnutrition is widespread, with an estimated 825,000 children affected in 2026 amid declining access to treatment.

Mortality criterion has largely been met in localized camps but remains unverified due to obvious reasons. It seems that "FEWS NET" are compiling further data from the relevant sources like, "UNICEF" & "World Food Programme" or local partner, if any, to reach any confirmation before declaring it as "Famine-affected region", However the progress of these humanitarian teams has been hindered by ongoing conflict. I believe the community's forecast is right on spot. &#x20;

### Background and Geopolitics

**S**udan has been ravaged by civil war that's going on since 2023. Its capital Khartoum is largely deserted and in ruins. It is estimated that overall 14 million people have been displaced through violence, genocide and conditions closer to famine. The conflict is between the two belligerents, the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF), country's de facto government; and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF), a rich and well equipped militia. Various global powers are allied with two sides. RSF receives weapons, money and mercenaries from United Arab Emirates (UAE), which UAE obviously danies, while the SAF gets its weapons and money from Egypt, Saudi Arabia, Qatar and Turkey. &#x20;

### US's push for peace and aid

Massad Boulos, an Advisor to the Trump on Arab and Middle Eastern Affairs, has proposed a humanitarian truce. He also stated that Washington is pushing for a comprehensive peace plan with unhindered humanitarian access. The remark came amid of growing UN concern over the detention of humanitarian workers in Sudan.

### Famines, weather and role of wars;

Famines are rare to occur, although the situation in Sudan is different or maybe precarious which has the potential for developing into a Famine. The wars are playing a big role here, Russia Ukraine war has disrupted the flow of grain to the world, approx 10% of world's wheat, 16% of corn and 13% of barley flows out of Ukraine. US - Iran war has catastrophic effects too, with strait of hormuz being closed from which majority of agricultural products flow, like urea, ammonia , phosphate and sulfur. Hormuz alone accounts for 30% fertilizer. Combining the effects of civil war with other major global conflicts which are affecting the cycles of crops and causing disruption to the global commodities can really increase the chance of Famine in the region that is almost devastated. I think there is a chance for a famine to occur, one can argue that the harvest season is around the corner (October to January) and can alleviate the pain, though the rainfall in Sudan has been below average which is bad news for the harvest season. So the chance for famine is not minuscule. Data shows that various indicators are turning red, like oil on water indicator that tracks ships on global waters, with Hormuz and Red sea both closed and on the top of that Saudi's east west pipeline is also out of service due to the Houthis attack on refinery in Yanbu. Experts say that it will likely take 3 to 6 weeks for refinery to get back to production, however refineries coming back online is of no use considering "bab al mandab" remains under the control of Houthis. In addition, Ukraine attack on Russian oil refineries has taken some oil out of the market too and this will likely make things worse, particularly for the poor and emerging economies. Trump is reluctant to attack Iran or even Houthis, he reportedly declined Saudis' crown prince call for attack on Houthis, This likely indicates that Trump is cautious as elections are approaching and he doesn't want to stir up trouble but sadly the damage has been done and Iran would likely play the hardball here as their goal is to put maximum pressure on Trump through economic squeeze.&#x20;
